Router-Sub Interface Concept:
-----------------------------
    we already know:
    - switch adds the VLAs tag
    - Router receives the tagged frame

    Now think about the router:
    ---------------------------
    - the router has only one physical interface: GigabitEthernet0/0
    - But we have: VLAN 10, VLAN 20

    How can one physical interface have two different IP address:
    -------------------------------------------------------------
    Example:
    192.168.10.1 AND 192.168.20.1

    Normally, one interface has one IP address
    So how is the router going to do this ?
    >> It creates subinterfaces

    Instead of one interface, the router logically divides it:
    ----------------------------------------------------------
        GigabitEthernet0/0
            |
            +--------------------+
            |                    |
         G0/0.10             G0/0.20
     VLAN 10 Gateway      VLAN 20 Gateway
    192.168.10.1          192.168.20.1

    >> Notice that there is still only one physical cable, but the router behaves as if it has
       separate logical interfaces for each VLAN




















